// THE EXPLICIT TRAPPING SAMPLE -- the one place in samples/ where `in Trapping`
// is the feature under test, not a convenience. Everywhere else the corpus
// posture is: PROVE bounds (Exact, no annotation) or DECLARE wrap semantics
// (`in Wrapping`). Trapping means: this arithmetic is allowed to be partial,
// and overflowing it ABORTS the process at the faulting instruction.
//
// x = i32::MAX, then x + 1 -> the emitted add is followed by an overflow check
// that executes ud2, so the process dies with STATUS_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION
// (0xC000001D) and the final exit_process(70) is NEVER reached. The companion
// canary (arithmetic/runtime_trapping_overflow_traps) runs this shape and
// asserts the process did NOT exit 70. This sample deliberately carries no
// exit annotation for the harness: it cannot exit cleanly by design.
//
// The compiler also WARNS here (2026-07-18): the interval analysis proves the
// increment ALWAYS overflows, and an unconditional trap is worth flagging --
// but only flagging (owner: no compelling reason to forbid it). The warning
// appearing in this sample's compile output is expected and correct.
use omega_language_std::console;
data Main { console: Console; x: i32 in Trapping; }
machine Main::main(&mut self) {
    self.x = 2147483647;
    self.x = self.x + 1;
    // unreachable: the increment above traps.
    self.console.exit_process(70);
}
